 the same diagonal? \n                  a3: \n                    diagonal up-right: b4, c5, d6, e7, f8\n                    diagonal down-left: not possible (a is leftmost)\n                    diagonal down-right: b2, c1\n                    diagonal up-left: not possible.\n                So a4 is not on a diagonal from a3? \n                  a3 and a4: same file -> not diagonal. \n                Therefore, bishop cannot capture.\n\n              King: \n                The white king can capture the knight if it moves to a4? \n                  But the king is at c3, and a4 is two files left and one rank up: \n                    c3 to a4: \n                      files: c->a: 2 left\n                      ranks: 3->4: 1 up -> that\'s a knight move? no, for king it\'s two steps. \n                  The king moves one square at a time. \n                  So to get to a4, the king would have to move: \n                    c3 -> b3 -> a3 -> a4? \n                  But in one move, the king can only move one square. \n                  So from c3, the king can move to b3, b4, c4, d3, d4, etc. but not directly to a4.\n\n                Therefore, the king cannot capture the knight in one move.\n\n            So white cannot capture the knight.\n\n          Therefore, white must move the king.\n\n          Since there are safe squares, white can escape.\n\n        However, even if white escapes, we have gained a tempo and put pressure on the white king.\n\n        But note: the white king is very exposed and we might have a mating net? \n\n        Alternatively, we might consider if there is a checkmate.\n\n        After N: c5-a4, the white king is in check. \n          If white has no legal moves to escape, then it\'s checkmate.\n\n        But we just saw that white has several escape squares. \n\n        Therefore, it\'s not checkmate.\n\n        However, it\'s a very strong move because:\n          - It forces the white king to move, which might lead to further attacks.\n          - The white king is already in a dangerous position.\n\n        Now, let\'s consider the other knight move: N: c5-e4.\n\n          After N: c5-e4:\n            The knight attacks the white king at c3? \n              e4 to c3: \n                2 left, 1 down -> valid knight move -> yes.\n\n          White king must escape.\n\n          Escape squares: \n            b2, b3, b4, c2, c4, d2, d3, d4.\n\n          Check which are attacked:\n\n            b2: \n              by knight at e4? \n                e4 to b2: \n                  3 left, 2 down -> not knight move (knight: 2 and 1, or 1 and 2). \n                So no.\n              by other black pieces? \n                black pawn at d5: attacks c4 and e4 -> not b2.\n                black king: too far.\n              so safe.\n\n            b3: \n              e4 to b3: \n                3 left, 1 down -> not knight move.\n              safe.\n\n            b4: \n              e4 to b4: 3 left, 0 -> no.\n              safe.\n\n            c2: \n              e4 to c2: 2 left, 2 down -> not knight move.\n              safe.\n\n            c4: \n              attacked by black pawn at d5? yes.\n\n            d2: \n              e4 to d2: 1 left, 2 down -> valid knight move -> so d2 is attacked.\n\n            d3: \n              e4 to d3: 1 left, 1 down -> not knight move.\n              but is it attacked by anything else? \n                black pawn at d5: doesn\'t attack d3 (pawns attack diagonally, not straight).\n              so safe? \n              However, note: the black knight at e4 attacks d2 and f2? \n                d2: 1 left, 2 down -> valid.\n                f2: 1 right, 2 down -> valid.\n                but d3: not attacked by knight.\n\n            d4: \n              e4 to d4: 1 left, 0 -> no.\n              safe.\n\n          So attacked squares: \n            c4 (by pawn) and d2 (by knight).\n\n          Therefore, safe squares: b2, b3, b4, c2, d3, d4.\n\n          White can escape.\n\n        So both knight moves (a4 and e4) put the king in check but do not deliver checkmate.\n\n        However, they are strong moves.\n\n        Now, let\'s consider other moves.\n\n        We also have the black king moves. The black king is at c6. \n          It can move to: b5, b6, c7, d6, d7.\n\n        Why would we move the king?\n          The black king is also somewhat exposed. \n          For example, if white plays ... (but it\'s black\'s turn) ... but after black moves, white might attack.\n\n          Currently, the white pawn at b4 is attacking c5. \n          The black king is at c6, so if the black king moves to d6, then the white pawn at b4 is still attacking c5, but that doesn\'t directly threaten the king.\n\n          However, note that the black king is on c6 and the white king is on c3. They are on the same file. \n          If the black king moves to c5, that would be moving into the "line of fire" of the white pawn at b4? \n          But c5 is attacked by the white pawn at b4 (because b4 pawn captures on c5). \n          So if black king moves to c5, it would be captured by the pawn? \n          However, moving the king into check is illegal. \n          So black cannot move the king to c5 because it is attacked by the white pawn.\n\n          Similarly, the black king cannot move to c4? but c4 is not in the list of king moves (the king moves are only to adjacent squares, and c4 is two ranks down from c6 -> not adjacent).\n\n          The king moves in the list: \n            K: c6-b5, c6-b6, c6-c7, c6-d6, c6-d7.\n\n          Check if these are safe:\n\n            b5: \n              attacked by white pawn at b4? \n                white pawn at b4: captures on a5 and c5 -> not b5.\n              attacked by white bishop at a3? \n                a3 to b5: \n                  diagonal: a3->b4->c5->d6->... but b5 is not on the same diagonal? \n                  a3: file a, rank 3 -> \n                  b5: file b, rank 5 -> \n                  difference: file 1, rank 2 -> not equal -> not diagonal.\n              attacked by white king? \n                white king at c3: \n                  adjacent squares: ... b4 is adjacent, but b5 is two ranks up and one file left -> not adjacent.\n              so b5 is safe.\n\n            b6: \n              attacked by white pawn at b4? \n                b4 pawn: moves forward to b5, and captures on a5 and c5 -> not b6.\n              by bishop: \n                a3 to b6: \n                  file: 1, rank: 3 -> not equal -> not diagonal.\n              so safe.\n\n            c7: \n              attacked by white pawn? no.\n              by bishop: \n                a3 to c7: \n                  file: 2, rank: 4 -> not equal -> not diagonal.\n              so safe.\n\n            d6: \n              attacked by white pawn at b4? no (b4 pawn attacks c5, not d6).\n              by bishop: \n                a3 to d6: \n                  file: 3, rank: 3 -> equal -> yes, diagonal! \n                  a3, b4, c5, d6 -> so d6 is on the diagonal from a3.\n                Therefore, d6 is attacked by the white bishop at a3.\n\n              So d6 is not safe.\n\n            d7: \n              by bishop: \n                a3 to d7: \n                  file: 3, rank: 4 -> not equal -> not diagonal.\n              safe.\n\n          Therefore, the king moves to d6 are illegal (because it would be moving into check). \n          So the valid king moves are: b5, b6, c7, d7.\n\n        Why move the king? \n          It might be to get out of potential danger, but currently the king is not in immediate danger. \n          However, the white bishop at a3 is pointing towards the black king\'s position? \n          Currently, the black king is at c6, and the bishop is at a3. \n          The bishop attacks d6 (as we saw) but not c6? \n            a3 to c6: \n              file: 2, rank: 3 -> not equal -> not diagonal.\n          So c6 is safe.\n\n          But