nks (since it\'s already on rank 1). So 4 moves. So Black\'s pawn would promote in 3 moves, White king takes 4. So the pawn would promote first. So pushing h4 to h3 is good.\n\nBut wait, maybe the White king can take a diagonal route. For example, d1 -> e2 -> f3 -> g4 -> h5... but in this case, the pawn is moving down, so maybe not. Wait, the White king needs to intercept the pawn. Let\'s see:\n\nIf Black plays h4-h3, then h3-h2, h2-h1=Q.\n\nWhite king starts at d1.\n\nAfter h4-h3 (move 1), White king can go d1-e2 (move 1)\n\nh3-h2 (move 2), White king e2-f3 (move 2)\n\nh2-h1=Q (move 3), White king f3-g4 (move 3). So White king is at g4 when pawn promotes. So the pawn promotes safely.\n\nThus, the h-pawn can promote before White king can stop it. So pushing h4 to h3 seems good.\n\nAnother pawn: a5. If Black pushes a5 to a4, then a3, a2, a1. White king from d1 to a1: needs to move left 3 files (d to c, b, a), so 3 moves. Black pawn needs 4 moves (a5->a4, a3, a2, a1). So White king would arrive at a1 in 3 moves, but pawn takes 4. So White king could stop it. For example:\n\na5-a4 (1), White d1-c2 (1)\n\na4-a3 (2), White c2-b3 (2)\n\na3-a2 (3), White b3-a4 (3) — but pawn is at a2, White king at a4, then pawn moves a2-a1=Q (4), but White king could capture on a2 if it\'s there. Wait, maybe not. Let\'s see:\n\nAfter a5-a4 (B), White Kd1-c2\n\na4-a3 (B), Kc2-b3\n\na3-a2 (B), Kb3xa2 — White king captures the pawn. So the a-pawn would be stopped.\n\nThus, the a-pawn is less likely to promote.\n\nSimilarly, d5-d4: pushing the d-pawn. From d5 to d1 is 4 ranks. White king is at d1, so if pawn reaches d2, White king can capture on d2. So d5-d4, then d4-d3, d3-d2. At d2, White king can take. So unless Black king supports it, this pawn might not promote.\n\nBut Black\'s king is at c4. Let\'s see if it can support the d-pawn.\n\nIf Black plays d5-d4, then White king might move towards it. But the Black king could go to c4-d3 or something.\n\nAlternatively, the g7 and h7 pawns: pus